<p>The Democratic Republic of Congo confirmed a new Ebola outbreak in eastern Ituri province; the health ministry said 80 people had died by late Friday. Africa CDC earlier reported 246 suspected cases and 65 deaths, mainly in the Mongwalu and Rwampara health zones. The outbreak is Congo’s 17th since Ebola was first identified in the country in 1976. Uganda reported one Ebola case, a 59-year-old Congolese man who was admitted to a Kampala hospital on May 11 and died three days later. Ugandan officials described it as an imported case from Congo and said no local case had been confirmed. Africa CDC said preliminary laboratory results detected Ebola virus in 13 of 20 samples and suggested a strain other than Ebola Zaire, while Uganda said the dead man tested positive for the Bundibugyo strain. Africa CDC said it would convene an urgent coordination meeting with Congo, Uganda, South Sudan and global partners to reinforce cross-border surveillance, preparedness and response. The WHO said it had sent a team to help Congo investigate the outbreak and collect samples.</p><img src='https://kagiproxy.com/img/Irme8-Qa1p4_l7F2u5tupxL3BA8H7s5sk5wGsAwBt3vvRzqPwHtPXE9yuDOlokOiWCmvi6viCmaxuDN5HIjPSBPTIYqnLFNwsV5Ygh3VkUV2uGDlAzfqed1ZAmWEL0O2VlulDatkhCTrY8NbPqSQ-TkhgcrzfWkmsGhJq4C1UQtw1SXxfpHtBZUod0IgHTOB7M7U0Q' alt='An Ebola treatment center in Mangima, Democratic Republic of Congo, in 2019.' /><br /><h3>Highlights:</h3><ul><li>Border geography: Ituri province borders Uganda and South Sudan.</li><li>Bunia watch: Africa CDC said suspected cases in Bunia, the provincial capital near Uganda, were still pending confirmation in its first statement.</li><li>Vaccine limits: WHO said Congo had a stockpile of treatments and about 2,000 Ervebo vaccine doses, but Ervebo is effective against the Ebola Zaire strain.</li><li>Recent outbreak: The latest outbreak was reported about five months after Congo’s previous Ebola outbreak was declared over following 43 deaths.</li></ul><blockquote>Africa 